Migration flow in bullet points
Started with an existing on-prem/local Kubernetes platform. We did not create a completely different application for AWS. The same Card Processing microservices and Kubernetes concepts were migrated.
Kept infrastructure and application deployment separated. Terraform owns AWS infrastructure; Helm owns Kubernetes application resources.
Built AWS networking with Terraform. We created the VPC, public/private subnets across availability zones, Internet Gateway, NAT/routing and the networking required by EKS.
Provisioned EKS using Terraform. The cluster was dev-card-processing-eks, with managed worker nodes placed in private subnets.
Configured IAM access. Terraform created the required cluster/node IAM resources, and we authenticated from our Windows development machine using the terraform-admin AWS CLI profile.
Connected VS Code/Windows to EKS. AWS CLI generated/updated the kubeconfig entry for the EKS cluster.
Validated EKS before deploying applications. kubectl get nodes showed both AWS worker nodes Ready, followed by validation of kube-system components.
Reused our existing Helm chart. Instead of maintaining separate manifests for AWS, we introduced values-eks.yaml for environment-specific overrides.
Changed the storage implementation. Local Kubernetes used local storage; EKS uses dynamically provisioned AWS EBS volumes.
Installed/configured the EBS CSI integration through Terraform. This required the EKS OIDC/IAM integration and CSI add-on.
Deployed the complete Card Processing stack through Helm. Payment API, Card Processor, Fraud Service, Audit Service, MySQL StatefulSet, Services, HPA and DB initialization Job ran inside the card-processing namespace.
Troubleshot real migration problems. We fixed pending PVCs, a Service selector mismatch that produced no endpoints, and a DB initialization mismatch where the table was created in auditdb while the application expected carddb.
Performed end-to-end testing. Payment and fraud flows worked, Audit Service connected to MySQL, and /history successfully returned stored transactions.
Proved persistence. We inserted TXN-EKS-001, restarted the Audit Service Deployment, and retrieved the same record afterward.
Committed the EKS-specific Helm work to Git on feature/aws-eks-migration, keeping generated files and real secrets out of Git.
3. How VS Code → kubectl → EKS works

This part is particularly useful to explain in an interview.

You are not running kubectl inside AWS.

kubectl runs locally from the VS Code terminal on your Windows machine:

┌───────────────────────────────────┐
│ Windows Laptop                    │
│                                   │
│ VS Code                           │
│   └── Integrated Terminal         │
│        ├── aws.exe                │
│        ├── kubectl.exe            │
│        ├── helm.exe               │
│        └── terraform.exe          │
└────────────────┬──────────────────┘
                 │
                 │ HTTPS :443
                 ▼
        EKS Kubernetes API
                 │
                 ▼
        Kubernetes resources
                 │
          ┌──────┴───────┐
          ▼              ▼
      Worker-1        Worker-2
          │              │
          └──── Pods ────┘

First we authenticated the AWS CLI:

set AWS_PROFILE=terraform-admin


aws sts get-caller-identity --profile terraform-admin

Then generated/updated the Kubernetes configuration:

aws eks update-kubeconfig ^
  --region us-east-1 ^
  --name dev-card-processing-eks ^
  --profile terraform-admin

That updated:

C:\Users\Admin\.kube\config

Conceptually:

AWS CLI
   │
   │ discovers EKS cluster
   ▼
C:\Users\Admin\.kube\config
   │
   │ cluster endpoint/context/auth configuration
   ▼
kubectl
   │
   │ HTTPS request
   ▼
EKS API Server

Therefore when we ran this inside the VS Code terminal:

kubectl get nodes

the command travelled to the EKS API server and returned the AWS worker nodes:

ip-10-0-11-241.ec2.internal   Ready
ip-10-0-12-153.ec2.internal   Ready

Similarly:

kubectl get pods -n card-processing

was querying Kubernetes running in AWS — not the old Vagrant cluster.

